1
00:00:00,630 --> 00:00:03,920
A YubiKey has one feature that no script can fake.

2
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:05,040
Touch.

3
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:08,540
The key waits for a finger, to prove a human is there.

4
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:10,270
That is great for security.

5
00:00:10,410 --> 00:00:12,300
And terrible for automated testing.

6
00:00:13,120 --> 00:00:17,680
I am building SCA, a simple certificate authority, in bash.

7
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,250
Its signing key lives on a YubiKey,

8
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:25,700
and I want every release tested automatically on real hardware.

9
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:28,860
That includes the cases that wait for a person:

10
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:32,740
touch policies, FIDO logins, and one time passwords.

11
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,190
So the test suite needed a finger.

12
00:00:35,990 --> 00:00:36,870
So I built one.

13
00:00:36,990 --> 00:00:39,960
This small printed box holds two YubiKeys.

14
00:00:39,990 --> 00:00:42,610
A pogo pin rests on each touch disk.

15
00:00:42,990 --> 00:00:45,960
A microcontroller grounds the pin on command,

16
00:00:46,150 --> 00:00:48,380
and the key reads it like a finger.

17
00:00:48,990 --> 00:00:50,670
From a test, it is one line.

18
00:00:51,560 --> 00:00:56,320
I built it in a loop: measure, model, print, fix.

19
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:59,740
Every part was measured with calipers,

20
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:03,060
and the whole box is one OpenSCAD file.

21
00:01:03,080 --> 00:01:07,220
One lesson: a pin resting on the key jams it,

22
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:11,610
so a small slide bar lifts the pin before the key moves.

23
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,100
The firmware is under two hundred lines of C.

24
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:18,570
And it works: six touches out of six were accepted.

25
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:24,460
And it is cheap: the parts came to about forty three dollars.

26
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:26,480
Most come in packs,

27
00:01:26,810 --> 00:01:30,850
so after one build you still have ninety eight pogo pins,

28
00:01:31,150 --> 00:01:35,590
three spare microcontrollers, and hundreds of resistors.

29
00:01:35,860 --> 00:01:38,370
The second fixture is almost free.

30
00:01:38,940 --> 00:01:41,630
One rule: use test keys only.

31
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:45,660
This box fakes human presence, and that is the whole point,

32
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:48,210
so never put a real key in it.

33
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:52,200
The model, the firmware and the parts list are all open,

34
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:53,690
in the SCA repository.

35
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:57,590
Real hardware, real touch, and no human required.
